  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal agencies obligated $559B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $286B, making defense the largest single agency by contract spending. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $68.4B and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $58.7B, together accounting for a large share of non-defense obligations. The Department of Energy obligated $48.4B and the General Services Administration obligated $20.6B, showing significant activity outside the traditional defense and homeland portfolios. The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $15.5B, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $10.9B, and the Department of Transportation obligated $9.6B. The Department of Agriculture obligated $7.3B and the Department of Justice obligated $7B. These ten agencies together illustrate where federal contract dollars are concentrated in FY2026 to date and how the $559B total is distributed across major departments.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies account for the largest share of FY2026 contract obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest, with $286B in obligations. The Department of Veterans Affairs follows with $68.4B and the Department of Homeland Security follows with $58.7B. The Department of Energy and the General Services Administration obligate $48.4B and $20.6B respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How do other major departments compare in FY2026 contract obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligates $15.5B and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligates $10.9B. The Department of Transportation obligates $9.6B, the Department of Agriculture obligates $7.3B, and the Department of Justice obligates $7B. These figures complete the top ten agency rankings against the $559B total."
      }
    ]
  },
